<speak>
<p>For our guiding case, u equals three, Node six gave an exact identity: E of omega divided by c of omega equals E of one divided by c of one. Here omega is a primitive cube root of unity. Now use the all ones congruence from Node five. Modulo one plus i, E has the same coefficients as J. We will show that these two equal normalized quantities would have different residues.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Recall J of P equals one plus P, through P to the eighth. At one, all nine terms become one, and their sum is nine. At omega, group the nine terms into three copies of one plus omega plus omega squared. Each copy has complex sum zero, so J of omega is exactly zero.<break time="700ms"/>Consequently, modulo one plus i, E of one has residue one, while E of omega has residue zero. The denominators remain invertible in the reduction ring, as established in Node six. In particular, c of one is positive or negative three, which has residue one. Thus E of one divided by c of one has residue one. E of omega divided by c of omega has residue zero. But these normalized quantities are equal. Zero and one are distinct in our reduction ring: this is a contradiction. It rules out u equal to three, so no circulant Hadamard matrix of order thirty six exists.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>When u has several distinct prime divisors, there are more evaluations. For general odd u greater than one, let I be the set of distinct primes dividing u. For each subset S, use zeta sub S, the product of the corresponding prime roots of unity. The empty subset gives one. Node six gives exact identities between alternating products. Expand those products, use the sign divisibilities, and cancel in characteristic zero before reducing modulo one plus i. This yields an alternating sum: sum over S of negative one to the size of S, times E of zeta sub S divided by c of zeta sub S. Call this sum script S. Its residue is zero.<break time="700ms"/>The smaller nodes give the exact expansions and cancellations. With several primes, these product identities replace the two input picture; we do not assume a common rotation between every pair of inputs. The final node combines this vanishing sum with the all ones congruence to rule out every odd u greater than one.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
